<BaseLayout
  title="Understanding Your Speed Test Results — PTCL Speed Test"
  description="What do download speed, upload speed, ping and jitter actually mean? A plain-English guide to reading your speed test results and what counts as good."
  canonical="/guides/understanding-your-results"
>
  <section class="page-head">
    <div class="container">
      <p class="breadcrumbs"><a href="/">Home</a> / <a href="/guides">Guides</a> / Understanding Your Results</p>
      <h1>Understanding Your Speed Test Results</h1>
      <p class="lead">
        Four numbers, four different stories. Here's how to read each one — and what
        "good" actually looks like for a home connection in Pakistan.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section">
    <div class="container prose">
      <h2>Download speed (Mbps)</h2>
      <p>
        Download speed is how fast data travels <em>to</em> your device. It is measured in
        megabits per second (Mbps) and it is the number your PTCL package advertises. It
        decides how fast web pages load, how smoothly videos stream, and how long files
        take to download.
      </p>
      <p>
        One common confusion: <strong>megabits are not megabytes</strong>. A 10&nbsp;Mbps
        connection downloads at roughly 1.25&nbsp;MB per second (divide by 8). So a
        1&nbsp;GB file takes about 13 minutes at 10&nbsp;Mbps, not 100 seconds.
      </p>

      <h2>Upload speed (Mbps)</h2>
      <p>
        Upload speed is how fast data travels <em>from</em> your device. On PTCL DSL it is
        usually a small fraction of your download speed — often 1&nbsp;Mbps or less. It
        matters most for video calls, online classes, sending large files, livestreaming
        and cloud backups. If people on your Zoom calls say you freeze or sound robotic,
        low upload speed is the usual suspect. Fibre connections have far better upload
        speeds than DSL.
      </p>

      <h2>Ping / latency (ms)</h2>
      <p>
        Ping is the time a signal takes to go to the server and back, measured in
        milliseconds (ms). Lower is better:
      </p>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>Under 20 ms</strong> — excellent. Everything feels instant.</li>
        <li><strong>20–50 ms</strong> — good. Browsing, calls and gaming all feel fine.</li>
        <li><strong>50–100 ms</strong> — acceptable for browsing and calls; competitive gamers will notice lag.</li>
        <li><strong>Over 100 ms</strong> — poor. Video calls feel delayed, games feel sluggish.</li>
      </ul>
      <p>
        Ping depends mostly on distance and routing, not on your package speed. A
        100&nbsp;Mbps connection can still have bad ping to a far-away game server.
      </p>

      <h2>Jitter (ms)</h2>
      <p>
        Jitter is how much your ping jumps around. A connection with a steady 40&nbsp;ms
        ping feels better than one that swings between 20 and 120&nbsp;ms — even though
        the average looks similar. Under 30&nbsp;ms of jitter is fine for calls and
        gaming; over 50&nbsp;ms and you'll hear stuttering and see freezing.
      </p>

      <h2>How much speed do common activities need?</h2>
      <div class="table-wrap">
        <table class="data">
          <thead>
            <tr><th>Activity</th><th>Download needed</th><th>Notes</th></tr>
          </thead>
          <tbody>
            <tr><td>Browsing &amp; email</td><td>1–5 Mbps</td><td>Light; ping matters more than speed</td></tr>
            <tr><td>YouTube / Netflix HD (1080p)</td><td>5–8 Mbps</td><td>Per stream</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Netflix / YouTube 4K</td><td>25 Mbps</td><td>Per stream</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Zoom / Teams video call</td><td>3 Mbps down, 3 Mbps up</td><td>Upload matters here</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Online gaming</td><td>3–6 Mbps</td><td>Low ping (&lt;50 ms) matters far more</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Large downloads / updates</td><td>As much as possible</td><td>10 GB takes ~2.5 hrs at 10 Mbps</td></tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>
      <p>
        Remember: these are <em>per activity</em>. Two HD streams plus a video call need
        roughly 15&nbsp;Mbps at the same time — plus headroom.
      </p>

      <h2>Why do my results change between tests?</h2>
      <p>
        Speed varies minute to minute. Wi-Fi interference, other devices using the
        connection, the test server's load, and network congestion all shift the numbers.
        Run the test a few times and look at the pattern rather than one result — and test
        over Ethernet for the truest picture of your line.
      </p>

      <div class="cta-band">
        <h2>Put it into practice</h2>
        <p>Run the test now and read your results with fresh eyes.</p>
        <a class="btn" href="/speed-test">Run the Speed Test</a>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>
</BaseLayout>
